  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 451 AM CDT Thu Aug 6 2026\n\nCurrent surface analysis places low over northeast Manitoba,\nwith cold front stretching to the southwest, while high remains\nover the Upper Midwest into parts of the Central Plains. Upper level\nanalysis places trough over the Midwest, with ridge pushing \nover the West Coast. A modest short wave continues to push \nthrough the Dakotas, but is only resulting in some additional \ncloud cover. \n\nFor today, west to southwesterly winds will bring warmer air\ninto the area, with high temperatures expected to be around 10\ndegrees above average. Winds start to become breezy over the\nwest, then a cold front enters northwest North Dakota early this\nafternoon, and drops to the southeast through the afternoon into\nthis evening. Winds will shift northwest and become a bit gusty.\nThe combination of winds and low humidity values over much of\nwestern North Dakota will lead to critical fire weather\nconditions, thus a Red Flag Warning is in place. \n\nFriday will see cooler though still seasonal temperatures over\nthe area in the wake of the aforementioned front as upper flow\nremains northwesterly, which continues through Saturday.\n\nAs we get into next week, upper flow remains west-northwesterly\nthough a variety of short waves are expected to move through.\nTherefore, low to medium (around 20 to 50 percent) chances for \nprecipitation enter the forecast.",
